Lab 13: Persistent Storage Setup for Application Logging
1. Prepare the directory on the worker node

hostPath uses the node's file system, so the directory must exist on the worker that will run the pods (not on the control plane):

bash
# on worker1
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/app-logs
sudo chmod 777 /mnt/app-logs        # lab only: lets any container user write
2. PersistentVolume ivolvepv

ivolvepv.yaml:

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: ivolvepv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 1Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteMany
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: ""            # no StorageClass
  hostPath:
    path: /mnt/app-logs
    type: DirectoryOrCreate
bash
kubectl apply -f ivolvepv.yaml

A PV is cluster-scoped, so it has no namespace.

3. PersistentVolumeClaim ivolvepvc

ivolvepvc.yaml:

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: ivolvepvc
  namespace: ivolve               # CHECK: the namespace where the app pods will run
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteMany               # must match the PV
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
  storageClassName: ""            # empty string = do not use any StorageClass (not even the default)
  volumeName: ivolvepv            # optional: bind explicitly to this PV
bash
kubectl apply -f ivolvepvc.yaml

Setting storageClassName: "" on both objects is what keeps the cluster from using a default StorageClass.

4. Verify
bash
kubectl get pv
kubectl get pvc -n ivolve
# ivolvepv   1Gi   RWX   Retain   Bound   ivolve/ivolvepvc
# ivolvepvc  Bound  ivolvepv  1Gi  RWX

kubectl describe pv ivolvepv
kubectl describe pvc ivolvepvc -n ivolve
